{"evt":"note_created","id":"note-mqvr2wl8lre","workspace":"D:\\PersonalProject\\zonoid","title":"First real ARC-AGI-3 ls20 run validates prompt-driven Zonoid memory generalization","summary":"First real Mode-B ON-arm run (ls20, --no-baseline, --max-steps 15, claude -p via local-cli stdio). Scorecard posted: https://arcprize.org/scorecards/57f40bf7-4f18-42c1-be7d-402782174a0f. Game outcome: 16 actions, 0/7 levels, score 0.0 — but that is purely a budget artifact: ls20 per-level baseline budgets are [22,123,73,84,96,192,186] (total 776), so 15-16 actions barely enters level 1. The KEY result is that the de-rigged, PROMPT-DRIVEN Zonoid memory protocol worked with zero harness rigging: the agent self-recalled via GET /search and self-recorded 12 distinct before->action->after transition notes via POST /overlay/note, then REUSED them to learn game mechanics — it deduced 'A1=UP, A2=DOWN, A3=LEFT, A4=RIGHT (each moves the block one 5x5 cell when the destination is open)' from its own recorded transitions, and by the final step used accumulated memory to track progress ('Block top went 29->24 this turn; UP working consistently') and reconcile contradictory earlier notes. This validates the generalization thesis: an agent given only a system-prompt memory protocol will build and exploit external state memory. TWO follow-ups: (1) --max-steps must be far larger (hundreds, matching the game's 776-action budget) for a real level attempt; (2) BUG/robustness: the agent found that backslashes in grid encodings silently broke /overlay/note JSON writes until it switched to forward-slashes — the note-write path or the protocol's grid-encoding guidance should handle backslash escaping safely.","category":null,"tags":[],"created_by":"main","valid_from":"2026-06-27T02:38:07.484Z","vec":[-0.04984693601727486,0.00372242066077888,-0.035230353474617004,-0.0020226305350661278,0.012322747148573399,-0.00703157763928175,-0.0461261086165905,0.11061723530292511,-0.009962795302271843,0.042452737689